{
    "_comment": [
        "Which @wordpress packages each WordPress release actually ships.",
        "",
        "This exists because the suite had only one answer to 'does the editor",
        "accept this markup?', and it was the answer for whichever library npm had",
        "most recently published - 10.3.0. No released WordPress ships 10.3.0.",
        "WordPress 7.0.2, the version the plugin declares as tested, ships 9.40.2.",
        "So the one validation the project trusted most was measuring a WordPress",
        "nobody runs. It also missed a real defect: see the core/cover element order",
        "in CODEX-REVIEW-RESPONSE.md.",
        "",
        "How each row was derived, so it can be checked and extended:",
        "  6.1 - 6.8  package.json at the matching wordpress-develop tag pins these",
        "             packages directly.",
        "  7.0.2      7.0 builds Gutenberg from a commit rather than from npm.",
        "             package.json carries `gutenberg.sha`; each package's version",
        "             comes from packages/<name>/package.json at that commit.",
        "",
        "Every version is repeated under `overrides` in the generated package.json,",
        "and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. Without it npm installs a second",
        "copy of @wordpress/blocks under @wordpress/block-editor, and of",
        "@wordpress/private-apis under both. Two copies of blocks means one copy",
        "registers the save-time filters and the other runs save(), so every block",
        "throws before it is validated; two copies of private-apis throws 'Cannot",
        "unlock an object that was not locked before' at import time. Neither failure",
        "says anything about the version under test.",
        "",
        "6.1 predates @wordpress/private-apis and correctly has no entry for it.",
        "",
        "Refresh with: bash bin/block-library-matrix.sh --show-mapping",
        "",
        "`unregistered` names the core blocks that release does not have. The plugin",
        "asks WP_Block_Type_Registry the same question at runtime and emits a",
        "fallback - core/details arrived in 6.3 - so the corpus has to be generated",
        "the way the plugin would generate it *there*, not the way it generates it",
        "on a current install. Without this, 6.1 and 6.2 report a core/details",
        "failure the plugin would never actually produce.",
        "",
        "`known-failures` names fixtures that are known not to validate on that",
        "release, with the reason. They do not fail the run - a regression would be",
        "invisible if every known problem stopped the suite. A known failure that",
        "starts *passing* does fail the run, so the list cannot quietly rot."
    ],
